#{[17592186059526 "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^(→) + 6b^(→) &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060046 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)i-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(10-28)i-(-3-16)j+(-21-40)k \\\\  & =-18i+19j-61k \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060194 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(6x-8)j+(0-2)k \\\\  & =⟨3,-(6x-8),-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-(6x-8))^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36{x}^{2}-96x+64+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77} & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+77 & =13 \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+64 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186083547 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186088272 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$"] [17592186101208 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186101209 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186106127 "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106128 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a\\cdot (3b\\times (-2c)) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =24 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106129 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106130 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106131 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120272 "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120275 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120281 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120527 "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120528 "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,3⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{t})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9t+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3(t+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129516 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b194b4882dcada56a10ec69f9dae80b363ea05efaae27385ceacc9a15ba3b56f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6+y=6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129517 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129519 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129626 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129739 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x=10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,1\\le z\\le 10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134065 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134099 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134136 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134137 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134138 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134139 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & =2z+0+0 \\\\  & =2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134140 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134368 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134369 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
